{
  "format": "pala-1 scitt-bridge statement identity vectors",
  "purpose": "What to refer to a PALA-1 SCITT statement by. statement_id is the SHA-256 of the statement with an empty unprotected header, in the one encoding a verifier accepts. Real crypto, fake entropy - never use this key outside a test vector.",
  "rule": [
    "A verifier accepts one encoding of a statement: tag 18 as the byte d2, a definite array of four (84), each byte string with its shortest definite length, and the protected header and the payload in the deterministic encoding of RFC 8949 section 4.2.1.",
    "The unprotected header is a map of a definite length with no label twice. What it holds, and how that is written, is not restricted: it is not signed, and a transparency service writes its receipts there with its own encoder.",
    "statement_id = SHA-256( d2 84 || bstr(protected) || a0 || bstr(payload) || bstr(signature) ): the statement with the unprotected header emptied.",
    "A statement whose unprotected header is the one byte a0 has statement_id equal to the SHA-256 of its bytes. A receipt attached later (RFC 9943, label 394, in the unprotected header) changes the bytes and their hash and leaves statement_id as it was.",
    "statement_id names the signed statement, not its content: the same payload signed twice with a non-deterministic algorithm is two statements."
  ],
